# Section 1.1 Designing for security and compliance: IAM and organization
# policies, encryption and key management, PII handling, data sovereignty,
# legal and regulatory compliance, project/dataset/table architecture for
# governance, development vs production. Per test 5, drill-only 12.
ITEMS = [
    # ---- Test 1 ----
    S(1, 2, "Identity and Access Management",
      "An analyst must run queries in project A against tables in project B. Which pair of roles grants the least access?",
      "Job User in A, Data Viewer on the tables",
      ["Data Editor in both projects", "Data Owner on the B dataset", "BigQuery Admin in project A and project B"],
      "Running a query creates a job, so the analyst needs bigquery.jobs.create in the project that runs it (BigQuery Job User). Reading the tables needs only BigQuery Data Viewer on them. Data Editor and Owner add write and sharing rights the analyst does not need."),
    S(1, 2, "Regional considerations",
      "Company policy says no new data resources may be created outside the EU. How is this enforced across every project?",
      "Organization policy on resource locations",
      ["IAM deny policies attached to every project", "VPC firewall rules that block non-EU regions", "Label on each EU dataset"],
      "The resource locations organization policy constraint (gcp.resourceLocations) limits where location-based resources can be created, and it inherits down the hierarchy. Firewall rules control traffic, not where data lives, and labels enforce nothing."),
    S(1, 3, "Data security",
      "A regulator requires that the company can revoke access to BigQuery data by disabling its own key. What should be used?",
      "CMEK in Cloud KMS",
      ["Google-owned default keys", "Column-level policy tags", "Client-side gzip compression"],
      "Customer-managed encryption keys live in the customer's Cloud KMS project. Disabling or destroying the key version makes the protected data unreadable. Default encryption uses Google-owned keys the customer cannot revoke, and policy tags control who reads a column, not the key."),
    M(1, 2, "Multi-environment use cases",
      "A team runs development and production pipelines on the same datasets today. Which two changes separate them best? (Choose two.)",
      ["Separate projects for dev and prod", "Service accounts per environment",
       "Table name suffixes for each environment", "One shared service account with Owner", "Different labels on dev jobs"],
      "Projects are the boundary for IAM, quotas and billing, so a separate production project keeps development jobs away from production data. A service account per environment means a dev job cannot write to prod. Suffixes and labels are naming conventions, not access boundaries."),
    S(1, 3, "Privacy",
      "Customer emails in a support table must stay joinable across tables, but analysts must never read them. Which approach fits?",
      "Deterministic encryption of the emails",
      ["Masking all but the last four characters", "Redacting the email column in every table", "Date shifting the records"],
      "Sensitive Data Protection's deterministic encryption turns the same email into the same token every time, so joins still work, and it can be reversed only with the key. Redaction removes the value, so nothing can be joined, and partial masking leaves different emails looking alike."),
    # ---- Test 2 ----
    S(2, 2, "Designing project, dataset and table architecture",
      "Finance and marketing tables share one dataset, and each team must see only its own tables. What is the simplest fix?",
      "Split them into one dataset per team",
      ["Add a row access policy to each table", "Rename the tables with a team prefix", "Move both teams to a new project"],
      "BigQuery grants are easiest to manage at the dataset level, so putting each team's tables in its own dataset lets one grant cover them. Row access policies filter rows inside a table, and prefixes do not control access."),
    S(2, 3, "Data security",
      "Data must be encrypted with keys held outside Google entirely, in a partner key manager. Which option meets this?",
      "Cloud External Key Manager",
      ["Cloud HSM keys", "Software keys in Cloud KMS", "Customer-supplied keys in a bucket"],
      "Cloud EKM uses keys stored in a supported external key manager, so the key material never sits in Google Cloud. Cloud HSM and software keys are customer-managed but still held in Google's Cloud KMS."),
    S(2, 2, "Identity and Access Management",
      "A Dataflow job reads a bucket and writes BigQuery tables. Which identity should hold those permissions?",
      "A dedicated worker service account",
      ["The developer's user account", "The Compute Engine default account", "A shared Owner account for all jobs"],
      "Dataflow workers act as the worker service account, so a dedicated account with only Storage Object Viewer on the bucket and Data Editor on the target dataset follows least privilege. The default Compute Engine account often has the broad Editor role."),
    M(2, 3, "Legal and regulatory compliance",
      "Health data in BigQuery must not be copied to projects outside the approved set, even with valid credentials. Which two controls help? (Choose two.)",
      ["VPC Service Controls perimeter", "BigQuery sharing egress restrictions",
       "Cloud Armor policy on the dataset", "Object versioning on exports", "Shorter time travel window"],
      "A VPC Service Controls perimeter blocks API calls that would move data from protected projects to projects outside it, even for a valid identity. When data is shared through BigQuery sharing, the publisher can restrict copy and export from the linked dataset. Cloud Armor protects web traffic, not BigQuery APIs."),
    S(2, 2, "Privacy",
      "Before loading a new vendor feed, a team wants to know which columns hold personal data. Which service helps most?",
      "Sensitive Data Protection discovery",
      ["Cloud Audit Logs for data access events", "BigQuery execution graph", "Cloud Monitoring uptime checks"],
      "Sensitive Data Protection inspects and profiles data to find infoTypes such as names, emails and card numbers. Audit logs record who read data, not what the data contains."),
    # ---- Drill only ----
    S(0, 1, "Identity and Access Management",
      "Fifty analysts need read access to a dataset, and staff change often. How should access be granted?",
      "To a Google group",
      ["To each user directly", "Through one shared login", "With a service account key per user"],
      "Granting a role to a group means joiners and leavers are handled by group membership, and the IAM policy stays short. Per-user grants drift, and shared logins break auditing."),
    S(0, 2, "Identity and Access Management",
      "A contractor needs to read one table only, not the rest of its dataset. What should be granted?",
      "Data Viewer on that table",
      ["Data Viewer on the dataset", "Metadata Viewer on the project", "Job User on the dataset"],
      "BigQuery supports IAM on individual tables and views, so Data Viewer on the single table limits the contractor to it. A dataset grant exposes every table in it, and Metadata Viewer shows schemas but not rows."),
    S(0, 2, "Data security",
      "Which statement about BigQuery data at rest is true by default?",
      "It is encrypted with Google-owned keys",
      ["It is stored unencrypted until CMEK is set", "It is encrypted only in multi-regions", "It needs a client library to encrypt"],
      "Google Cloud encrypts all customer data at rest by default with Google-owned and Google-managed keys. CMEK adds customer control over the key; it is not needed for encryption itself."),
    S(0, 3, "Data security",
      "A company wants its CMEK keys rotated on a schedule with no pipeline changes. What should it configure?",
      "Automatic rotation on the Cloud KMS key",
      ["New datasets with new keys each quarter", "A script that re-encrypts and re-exports tables", "Customer-supplied keys per load"],
      "Cloud KMS can rotate a symmetric key automatically by creating a new primary key version. New data is encrypted with it while older versions stay available to decrypt, so pipelines need no change."),
    S(0, 2, "Privacy",
      "Ages in a research extract must be generalised so no exact age is shown. Which de-identification method fits?",
      "Bucketing into ranges",
      ["Crypto hash", "Format-preserving encryption", "Character masking"],
      "Bucketing replaces a value with a range such as 30 to 39, keeping it useful for analysis while hiding the exact age. A hash or encryption produces tokens that cannot be grouped by age."),
    S(0, 3, "Privacy",
      "Patient visit dates must be hidden, but the gaps between one patient's visits must stay accurate. Which method fits?",
      "Date shifting per patient",
      ["Time extraction of the year", "Replacement with a fixed date", "Redaction of the date field"],
      "Date shifting moves dates by a random amount, and using a context such as the patient ID shifts all of one patient's dates by the same amount, so intervals survive. Keeping only the year loses the gaps between visits."),
    S(0, 2, "Regional considerations",
      "A BigQuery dataset was created in the US multi-region by mistake and must be in europe-west3. What must happen?",
      "Copy it to a new dataset in that region",
      ["Edit the location field in dataset settings", "Change the project's default region setting", "Add a regional label to the dataset"],
      "A dataset's location is fixed when it is created. Moving data means creating a dataset in the new location and copying the tables, for example with a dataset copy. No setting changes the location in place."),
    S(0, 2, "Legal and regulatory compliance",
      "Auditors ask who read rows from a sensitive BigQuery table last month. Where is that recorded?",
      "Data Access audit logs",
      ["Admin Activity audit logs", "Data quality scan results", "Dataflow job logs"],
      "Reads of data are Data Access events in Cloud Audit Logs. For BigQuery these logs are written by default; for many other services they must be turned on. Admin Activity logs record configuration changes such as a new dataset, not reads."),
    S(0, 3, "Designing project, dataset and table architecture",
      "Analysts need summary results from a dataset that also holds raw PII, without any access to the raw tables. What fits?",
      "Authorized view in a separate dataset",
      ["Grant on the raw dataset with a filter", "Table copy refreshed by hand", "Public dataset with a password"],
      "An authorized view is allowed to read the source tables, so users with access to the view's dataset see only its results. They need no permission on the raw dataset. Manual copies go stale and duplicate data."),
    S(0, 2, "Designing project, dataset and table architecture",
      "Salary columns must be hidden from most users of an HR table while other columns stay readable. What should be used?",
      "Column policy tags",
      ["Row access policy on the table", "A second project for HR", "Table expiration on the dataset"],
      "Column-level access control attaches policy tags to columns, and only principals with Fine-Grained Reader on the tag can read them. Row access policies filter rows, not columns."),
    S(0, 3, "Multi-environment use cases",
      "Testers need realistic data in development, but production holds customer PII. What is the best approach?",
      "Load a de-identified copy into development",
      ["Give testers read access to production", "Copy production tables as they are", "Let testers query production through a view"],
      "A de-identified copy, made with Sensitive Data Protection transforms, gives realistic shapes and volumes without exposing PII outside production. Any route that reads production data still exposes it to the testers."),
    M(0, 2, "Identity and Access Management",
      "A data platform team wants to stop anyone creating service account keys. Which two steps help? (Choose two.)",
      ["Enforce the disable key creation policy", "Use Workload Identity Federation for outside jobs",
       "Rotate keys every 90 days", "Store keys in a private bucket", "Email keys to admins only"],
      "The organization policy constraint that disables service account key creation blocks new keys everywhere below it. Workload Identity Federation lets workloads outside Google Cloud get short-lived credentials without keys. Rotating or hiding keys still leaves long-lived secrets in use."),
]
